::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve the system y=x+3 and y=7. {
= x=4 #Correct. Substitute y=7 into 7=x+3, then subtract 3.
~ x=3 #This uses the constant from the equation, not the solution.
~ x=7 #This is the y-value, not the x-value.
~ x=10 #This adds 7 and 3 instead of solving.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies both y>2x and y<9? {
~ (4,8) #This gives y=8, but 8 is not greater than 2(4)=8.
= (3,7) #Correct. 7>6 and 7<9, so both inequalities are true.
~ (5,11) #This is greater than 9, so it fails the second inequality.
~ (2,3) #This is less than 2(2)=4, so it fails the first inequality.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A line and a parabola intersect at (-1,2) and (3,6). What are the system solutions? {
~ x=2 and x=6 #These are y-values, not complete system solution inputs.
~ x=-1 only #This misses the second intersection.
= (-1,2) and (3,6) #Correct. System solutions are the points that satisfy both equations.
~ No solution #Two intersection points are given, so the system has solutions.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>How does y=(x-4)<sup>2</sup>+1 transform y=x<sup>2</sup>? {
~ Left 4 and up 1 #The inside sign is interpreted oppositely.
~ Right 4 and down 1 #The outside constant is positive, so the shift is up.
~ Left 1 and up 4 #This switches the horizontal and vertical shifts.
= Right 4 and up 1 #Correct. x-4 shifts right 4, and +1 shifts up 1.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=5x, what is f(g(2))? {
= 12 #Correct. g(2)=10, then f(10)=12.
~ 10 #This stops after evaluating g(2).
~ 7 #This applies f to 5 instead of applying g to 2 first.
~ 20 #This multiplies after adding instead of composing.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>For (f/g)(x), which inputs must be excluded? {
~ Inputs where f(x)=0 #A zero numerator is allowed in a quotient.
= Inputs where g(x)=0 #Correct. The denominator cannot equal zero.
~ All negative inputs #Negative values are not automatically excluded.
~ All real numbers #Only inputs that make the denominator zero are excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=2x-6. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=2x+6 #This does not reverse the order of operations.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-6)/2 #The sign should change when undoing -6.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x+6)/2 #Correct. Add 6 first, then divide by 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(2x-6) #Inverse function notation does not mean reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which check verifies that f and g are inverse functions? {
~ f(x)+g(x)=0 #This checks additive opposites, not inverse functions.
~ f(x)g(x)=1 #This checks reciprocals in special cases, not inverse functions.
~ f(x)=g(x) #Inverse functions do not usually have identical rules.
= f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x #Correct. Both compositions must return the original input.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What is the average rate of change from (1,5) to (4,17)? {
= 4 #Correct. (17-5)/(4-1)=12/3=4.
~ 3 #This is the change in input, not the rate.
~ 12 #This is the change in output before dividing.
~ 22 #This adds the output values.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>A context gives possible times t=-3 and t=8. Which value is viable? {
~ t=-3 only #Negative time before the starting point is not viable.
= t=8 only #Correct. The positive time value is viable in this context.
~ Both values #The negative time value violates the context.
~ Neither value #The positive time value is viable.
}
